narrative:
## Mathematical Safety Nets

Real math has constraints: you can't take the square root of a negative number, and log(0) is undefined. Option makes these constraints explicit.

### Safe Square Root

```xiom
fn safe_sqrt(n: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if n < 0 { return None; }
  let root = 0;
  while root * root <= n {
    if root * root == n { return Some(root); }
    root = root + 1;
  }
  return None;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(safe_sqrt(25).unwrap_or(-1));   // 5
  io.println(safe_sqrt(7).unwrap_or(-1));    // -1 (not a perfect square)
  io.println(safe_sqrt(-4).unwrap_or(-1));   // -1 (negative)
  return 0;
}
```

### Safe Logarithm (Power Check)

```xiom
fn safe_log(value: Int, base: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if value <= 0 || base <= 1 { return None; }
  let power = 0;
  let result = 1;
  while result < value {
    result = result * base;
    power = power + 1;
  }
  if result == value { return Some(power); }
  return None;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(safe_log(8, 2).unwrap_or(-1));   // 3 (2^3 = 8)
  io.println(safe_log(10, 2).unwrap_or(-1));  // -1 (not exact)
  io.println(safe_log(0, 2).unwrap_or(-1));   // -1 (invalid)
  return 0;
}
```

### Chaining Safe Operations

```xiom
fn compute(a: Int, b: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  let sqrt_a = safe_sqrt(a);
  if let Some(sa) = sqrt_a {
    let log_b = safe_log(b, 2);
    if let Some(lb) = log_b {
      return Some(sa + lb);
    }
  }
  return None;
}
```

